  <section id="patterns" aria-labelledby="patterns-title">
    <h2 id="patterns-title">Choose the right pattern for the job</h2>
    <table>
      <caption>Materialization strategies compared</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Pattern</th>
          <th scope="col">How it works</th>
          <th scope="col">Best for</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Mutable table (upsert/delete)</th>
          <td>Apply change events directly to a target table using merge logic.</td>
          <td>Serving layer caches, feature stores, operational analytics.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Streaming merge</th>
          <td>Continuous ingestion job (dbt incremental, Snowpipe, Flink) performs row-level MERGE as events arrive.</td>
          <td>Low-latency marts where freshness is minutes, not hours.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Append-only with views</th>
          <td>Store each event as a row, surface latest state via window functions.</td>
          <td>Auditable fact tables, debugging, ad-hoc comparisons.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Incremental batch</th>
          <td>Land CDC events in stages, then run scheduled merge statements.</td>
          <td>Warehouses that favor batch execution or have strict cost controls.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="merge" aria-labelledby="merge-title">
    <h2 id="merge-title">Build idempotent merge logic</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Stage events in a temporary table keyed by primary key and log position.</li>
      <li>Deduplicate to one row per key by ordering on the <strong>source log position</strong>
        (LSN/SCN/binlog position) — not <code>ts_ms</code>.
        <code>ts_ms</code> is a millisecond commit time and is not unique per key, so
        same-millisecond changes can tie and apply out of order; use the log position as
        the <strong>primary</strong> sort, with a within-transaction ordinal (Debezium
        <code>source.sequence</code>, or the row index) as the secondary key. If a position can
        repeat for a key (Oracle SCN, MySQL multi-row events), compare the (position, ordinal)
        pair. Keep <code>ts_ms</code> out of ordering entirely.</li>
      <li>Merge updates and inserts using the latest event per key (the <a href="{{ '/merge-cookbook/' | url }}">MERGE cookbook</a> has per-warehouse templates).</li>
      <li>Apply deletes as delete markers (a <code>deleted</code> flag plus the delete&rsquo;s log position)
        so a late, older update cannot bring the row back. Physically removing the row is safe only when
        nothing can replay older events.</li>
      <li>Record the processed offset so restarts can resume safely.</li>
      <li>Publish metrics: rows inserted, updated, deleted, skipped duplicates.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="callout">
      When duplicates land, the <em>last change wins</em> rule keeps results stable. Do not rely on arrival order or timestamps; rely on the
      source log position.
    </p>
    <p>
      Capture merge SQL in version control and tag it with the connector
      version it expects. This makes rollbacks trivial when envelope changes
      introduce new columns.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="sql" aria-labelledby="sql-title">
    <h2 id="sql-title">SQL templates you can adapt</h2>
    <details open>
      <summary>Snowflake MERGE</summary>
      <pre><code class="language-sql">-- staging.orders_src must already be deduped to ONE row per order_id
-- (latest by log position, e.g. LSN/SCN) before this MERGE runs.
-- Deletes are kept as delete markers (deleted = TRUE plus the delete's
-- position), so a replayed older update cannot resurrect the row. Readers
-- filter deleted = FALSE; amount/status are NULL on a delete, so those
-- columns must be nullable. Assumes src.lsn is unique per change for a key;
-- if it can repeat, compare the (lsn, seq) pair as in /partitioning/#recon.
-- If you add source_lsn to an existing table, backfill it first: a NULL
-- source_lsn makes tgt.source_lsn < src.lsn NULL, so the row is silently
-- never updated. Or compare COALESCE(tgt.source_lsn, -1) < src.lsn.
MERGE INTO analytics.orders tgt
USING staging.orders_src src
  ON tgt.order_id = src.order_id
WHEN MATCHED AND tgt.source_lsn < src.lsn THEN UPDATE SET
  amount = src.amount,
  status = src.status,
  deleted = (src.op = 'd'),
  source_lsn = src.lsn,
  processed_ts_ms = src.ts_ms  -- metric only, never used for ordering
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN
  INSERT (order_id, amount, status, deleted, source_lsn, processed_ts_ms)
  VALUES (src.order_id, src.amount, src.status, (src.op = 'd'), src.lsn, src.ts_ms);
</code></pre>
      <p class="muted">
        Why <code>source_lsn</code> and a delete marker, and what makes a position safe to compare:
        <a href="{{ '/partitioning/' | url }}#recon">Making Sinks Bulletproof</a>.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>BigQuery change-applier</summary>
      <pre><code class="language-sql">-- Full rebuild from the retained event history in staging. The delete is kept
-- as a delete marker (deleted = TRUE plus its position), exactly as in the
-- incremental targets above and below, so readers filter deleted = FALSE and
-- nothing that later re-applies older events can resurrect a deleted key.
-- amount/status are NULL on a delete, so those columns must be nullable.
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE analytics.orders AS
SELECT
  order_id,
  amount,
  status,
  (op = 'd') AS deleted,
  lsn AS source_lsn
FROM (
  SELECT *,
         -- order by log position (authoritative commit order); seq is the
         -- within-transaction ordinal for changes that share a position
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY order_id ORDER BY lsn DESC, seq DESC) AS rnk
  FROM staging.orders_src
)
WHERE rnk = 1;
</code></pre>
      <p class="muted">
        Untested: not run against a live BigQuery project. Check it against your own schema before use.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>dbt incremental model</summary>
      {% raw %}
      <pre><code class="language-sql">-- models/orders.sql. Written for Snowflake, BigQuery and PostgreSQL 15+
-- adapters that support incremental_strategy='merge'. The target needs
-- order_id, amount, status (nullable), deleted and source_lsn (never NULL).
{{
  config(
    materialized='incremental',
    incremental_strategy='merge',
    unique_key='order_id'
  )
}}

with source_events as (
  select order_id, amount, status, op, lsn, seq
  from {{ source('cdc', 'orders') }}
  {% if is_incremental() %}
  -- Bound the scan by the source log position already applied. It is >=, not >:
  -- re-reading the changes at the high-water position is harmless (the guard
  -- below drops them) and keeps a different key's change that shares that
  -- position. The same key at the same lsn is dropped by the strict guard, so
  -- if one key can have two changes at one position, store the ordinal and
  -- compare the pair. A strict
  -- bound is not safe if changes can land in this table out of order: the
  -- late row has a lower lsn than max(source_lsn) and is skipped for good.
  -- var('lsn_lookback') subtracts a margin (default 0) to re-read further back.
  where lsn &gt;= (
    select coalesce(max(source_lsn), 0) - {{ var('lsn_lookback', 0) }}
    from {{ this }}
  )
  {% endif %}
),

ranked as (
  select order_id, amount, status, op, lsn,
         -- order by log position (authoritative); seq is the within-transaction
         -- ordinal for changes that share a position
         row_number() over (
           partition by order_id
           order by lsn desc, seq desc
         ) as rnk
  from source_events
),

latest as (
  select order_id, amount, status, op, lsn
  from ranked
  where rnk = 1
)

-- The delete is not filtered out here. It becomes a delete marker row:
-- deleted = true plus the delete's position, so a replayed older update
-- cannot bring the key back. Readers filter deleted = false.
select
  l.order_id,
  l.amount,
  l.status,
  (l.op = 'd') as deleted,
  l.lsn as source_lsn
from latest l
{% if is_incremental() %}
-- The version guard. Keep a change only if the key is new or the change is
-- later in the log than the row already stored. A replay carries the same
-- position it had the first time, so it is dropped here.
left join {{ this }} t
  on t.order_id = l.order_id
where t.order_id is null
   or t.source_lsn &lt; l.lsn
{% endif %}
</code></pre>
      {% endraw %}
      <p class="muted">
        Untested: not run against a live warehouse. Two limits to know. The guard is evaluated
        before dbt issues the merge, so it assumes one run at a time per model (two concurrent runs
        can race). It also assumes <code>lsn</code> is a single number that is unique per change for a key;
        if it can repeat, or is a tuple (MySQL file and position, SQL Server LSNs), store the ordinal
        alongside it and compare the pair as in
        <a href="{{ '/partitioning/' | url }}#recon">Making Sinks Bulletproof</a>, and bound on
        a loader-assigned load id instead of <code>lsn</code>. Do not use
        <code>incremental_predicates</code> for the guard: it goes into the merge&rsquo;s
        <code>ON</code> clause, so a row that fails it is treated as new and inserted again.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section id="history" aria-labelledby="history-title">
    <h2 id="history-title">Keep history when you need it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Add <code>valid_from</code> and <code>valid_to</code> columns around each change.</li>
      <li>Close the previous version when a new update arrives.</li>
      <li>Expose current rows via a view that filters on <code>valid_to IS NULL</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This pattern powers <abbr title="slowly changing dimension">SCD</abbr> Type 2 tables without losing the benefits of CDC
      freshness.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="late-arrivals" aria-labelledby="late-arrivals-title">
    <h2 id="late-arrivals-title">Handle late-arriving and out-of-order events</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Track a <em>watermark</em> timestamp in your staging table to bound your replay budget; which version of a row wins is decided by log position, never by that timestamp.</li>
      <li>Store raw envelopes for N days so you can reprocess with updated merge logic.</li>
      <li>Document how to reconcile when the source database is manually patched (backfills, deletes).</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout">Late arrivals are usually replayed by ops teams—make the procedure idempotent so you can run it twice without fear.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="ops" aria-labelledby="ops-title">
    <h2 id="ops-title">Operational guardrails</h2>
    <details>
      <summary>Validate inputs</summary>
      <ul>
        <li>Reject events that miss primary keys or required columns.</li>
        <li>Track schema version and fail fast on incompatible changes.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Observe merge latency</summary>
      <ul>
        <li>Alert when staging tables age beyond the expected SLA.</li>
        <li>Keep a backlog gauge so you know when to scale compute.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Disaster recovery</summary>
      <ul>
        <li>Retain staging data long enough to replay the last full window.</li>
        <li>Store checkpoints (offset, batch id, timestamps) beside the target table.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>
    <p>
      Combine these runbooks with automated smoke tests that upsert and delete
      a synthetic record daily. The job should fail if materialization drifts.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="quality" aria-labelledby="quality-title">
    <h2 id="quality-title">Data quality checks before publishing</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Compare row counts between source and target using time-bounded windows.</li>
      <li>Verify key uniqueness and detect primary-key churn with a duplicate audit query.</li>
      <li>Validate soft deletes by ensuring delete markers propagate to downstream marts within SLA.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Embed these checks in orchestration (Airflow, Dagster, dbt) so they run
      on every deploy and after infrastructure maintenance.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="verification" aria-labelledby="verification-title">
    <h2 id="verification-title">Verification playbooks</h2>
    <div class="grid">
      <article>
        <h3>Pre-prod regression</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Run fixtures that upsert, delete, and reinsert entities to validate merge branches.</li>
          <li>Backfill a 1-hour slice from production CDC into staging and diff row-level hashes.</li>
          <li>Exercise late-arrival and out-of-order scenarios before promoting SQL changes.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Production spot checks</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Schedule canary merges on synthetic ids with deterministic payloads.</li>
          <li>Export change event samples weekly and replay them against a shadow environment.</li>
          <li>Rotate on-call owners through a monthly verification drill to keep docs fresh.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="callout">
      Treat verification as part of the deployment pipeline—pair SQL reviews with a matching verification checklist so no
      merge ships without evidence that the materialized view still behaves.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="scorecard" aria-labelledby="scorecard-title">
